WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:14.516
Catherine Abreu: We know with certainty
that fossil fuels need to be phased out.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:17.196
There's really no question
in the science at this point.

00:00:17.236 --> 00:00:20.356
Tessa Khan: The arguments
for transitioning away from fossil fuels

00:00:20.396 --> 00:00:21.556
at speed

00:00:21.596 --> 00:00:23.156
have never been more compelling.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.156
I think the responsibilities
of governments

00:00:25.156 --> 00:00:28.556
to accelerate that transition
have never been so acute.

00:00:28.596 --> 00:00:30.716
Laurence Tubiana:
It has to be quite radical.

00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:32.356
It could have been done slowly.

00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:34.756
But now we are running against time,

00:00:34.796 --> 00:00:37.716
and the climate impacts are now
not for tomorrow.

00:00:37.716 --> 00:00:38.916
It's for today.

00:00:38.916 --> 00:00:41.956
Hisham Mundol: The role of fossil fuels
between now and 2050

00:00:41.996 --> 00:00:43.596
is that of a necessary evil.

00:00:43.636 --> 00:00:47.156
Hongqiao Liu: So we have a very
complicated relationship with fossil fuels

00:00:47.196 --> 00:00:48.356
at the moment.

00:00:48.396 --> 00:00:51.076
It's like your ex-partner,
you want to get rid of them,

00:00:51.116 --> 00:00:52.676
but they stick around.

00:00:52.716 --> 00:00:56.716
Rebekah Shirley: In Africa, for instance,
where I live, it's a bit more complicated.

00:00:56.716 --> 00:00:59.756
Vijaya Ramachandran: I don't think
it's fair to poor countries

00:00:59.756 --> 00:01:01.556
to say you have to be renewable only,

00:01:01.596 --> 00:01:04.596
when the rich countries are doubling down
on oil, coal and gas.

00:01:04.596 --> 00:01:06.116
HL: We need to debate.

00:01:06.116 --> 00:01:07.756
We need we need that exchange.

00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:10.076
We need to understand each other's views

00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:14.156
and to start with the concept
of the dilemma that we’re facing.

00:01:14.196 --> 00:01:18.636
Zoë Knight: In my mind, the biggest
obstacle is the speed of collaboration.

00:01:18.676 --> 00:01:21.676
We speak at cross-purposes
with each other.

00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:25.396
Mary Robinson: We've got to deal with this
much more quickly than we're doing.

00:01:25.396 --> 00:01:26.956
How do we raise the temperature?

00:01:26.956 --> 00:01:30.116
How do we increase the energy for action,
the passion for action,

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.236
the heartfelt need for action
which will move us?

00:01:34.916 --> 00:01:37.716
[TED Countdown]

00:01:37.756 --> 00:01:40.756
[Dilemma Series]

00:01:42.676 --> 00:01:46.876
[How do we move from fossil fuels
to clean energy quickly and fairly?]

00:01:47.836 --> 00:01:50.996
Lindsay Levin: We're standing at a crucial
crossroads in history.

00:01:51.036 --> 00:01:54.316
We live in a world where our energy
system is overwhelmingly dependent

00:01:54.356 --> 00:01:55.876
on burning fossil fuels.

00:01:56.556 --> 00:01:59.636
What are the viable pathways
to a clean and safe future,

00:01:59.676 --> 00:02:02.276
and how do we collectively
navigate this transition?

00:02:03.476 --> 00:02:05.916
I'm Lindsay Levin,
co-founder of Countdown,

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:08.916
and this is the second installment
of our Dilemma Series.

00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:11.956
We know that credible people,
including many of you,

00:02:11.996 --> 00:02:15.116
disagree in some pretty fundamental
ways on this topic,

00:02:15.116 --> 00:02:17.236
the core of the climate challenge.

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:21.036
Recently at the Barbican Centre in London,
we brought together experts,

00:02:21.076 --> 00:02:24.676
activists and leading voices
in the climate space to speak,

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:28.116
listen and work together
as we figure out how to make progress

00:02:28.156 --> 00:02:29.836
despite our differences.

00:02:29.876 --> 00:02:33.236
While the need to get off fossil fuels
is straightforward and urgent,

00:02:33.276 --> 00:02:36.316
the pathway to doing it equitably
is anything but.

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:38.316
Here's TED science curator David Biello

00:02:38.356 --> 00:02:41.076
to frame the basic ideas
behind this dilemma.

00:02:41.076 --> 00:02:44.076
David Biello: Today, more
than 80 percent of modern energy

00:02:44.116 --> 00:02:46.436
comes from burning fossil fuels.

00:02:46.476 --> 00:02:49.356
That means more
than 40 billion metric tons,

00:02:49.396 --> 00:02:54.316
that's 40 gigatons, of CO2
gets dumped in the air each year.

00:02:54.356 --> 00:02:56.036
And that number is growing.

00:02:56.076 --> 00:02:57.516
At the same time,

00:02:57.516 --> 00:03:02.076
hundreds of millions of people
live with limited or no access

00:03:02.076 --> 00:03:03.836
to modern energy at all.

00:03:03.876 --> 00:03:06.516
That means they have
contributed almost nothing

00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:10.076
to the greenhouse gas pollution
causing the climate crisis.

00:03:10.116 --> 00:03:13.556
So do we stop fossil fuel exploration
and production entirely?

00:03:13.596 --> 00:03:16.316
Do we encourage it for certain
communities but not others?

00:03:16.316 --> 00:03:17.516
And if so, where?

00:03:17.556 --> 00:03:21.836
How do we actually get renewables to scale
and how do we pay for it all?

00:03:21.876 --> 00:03:24.436
These questions are incredibly complex,

00:03:24.476 --> 00:03:26.276
but the takeaway is clear.

00:03:26.276 --> 00:03:28.796
We need to eliminate fossil fuels

00:03:28.836 --> 00:03:32.476
and use what we must to ensure
that everyone benefits.

00:03:32.516 --> 00:03:35.676
Let's start with some facts and figures
about where we are today.

00:03:35.716 --> 00:03:37.036
Here's Adair Turner.

00:03:38.356 --> 00:03:42.756
Adair Turner: The International Panel
on Climate Change estimated in 2018

00:03:42.796 --> 00:03:45.996
that if we are to have
even a 50-percent chance

00:03:45.996 --> 00:03:49.876
of limiting global warming
to 1.5 degrees centigrade,

00:03:49.916 --> 00:03:56.636
we cannot emit more than 500 gigatons
of CO2 between 2020 and 2050.

00:03:57.156 --> 00:04:01.436
But actually, we're two years
into that 30-year period.

00:04:01.436 --> 00:04:04.156
We've been running
at 40 gigatons per year,

00:04:04.156 --> 00:04:07.716
so we've only got 420 gigatons left.

00:04:08.476 --> 00:04:11.236
That 420 gigatons would be emitted

00:04:11.276 --> 00:04:15.796
if we burnt 70 billion tons of coal

00:04:15.836 --> 00:04:19.636
plus 65 trillion cubic meters of gas

00:04:19.636 --> 00:04:23.396
plus 350 billion barrels of oil.

00:04:23.916 --> 00:04:29.956
But at the current rate we will have
burnt all of that in 12 years.

00:04:29.996 --> 00:04:36.636
We have got to find a way of leaving
the vast majority of coal, oil and gas

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:37.916
in the ground,

00:04:37.956 --> 00:04:42.796
even if it is in someone's interest
to dig it or drill it up.

00:04:43.156 --> 00:04:44.996
So what should we do?

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:48.316
In one area, the answer is clear.

00:04:48.756 --> 00:04:53.236
Renewable is now a cheaper way
to produce electricity than coal

00:04:53.276 --> 00:04:56.036
in almost all countries of the world,

00:04:56.036 --> 00:05:00.116
so we should build
no more coal electricity plants,

00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:02.436
no more coal mines.

00:05:02.436 --> 00:05:07.116
And we should not invest anything
in the extension of existing coal mines.

00:05:07.716 --> 00:05:10.756
For gas and oil, it is a bit trickier.

00:05:10.796 --> 00:05:14.316
International Energy Agency
suggests a distinction.

00:05:14.836 --> 00:05:19.756
It says that we should invest
nothing in exploration for

00:05:19.796 --> 00:05:24.956
or production from new oil and gas fields,

00:05:24.956 --> 00:05:27.396
but that it’s OK to invest something

00:05:27.436 --> 00:05:30.916
to maintain production
from existing fields.

00:05:31.556 --> 00:05:33.676
But there are some people
who would challenge

00:05:33.676 --> 00:05:36.516
the morality of that distinction,

00:05:36.556 --> 00:05:42.636
because if there's an absolute limit
to how much gas we can safely burn,

00:05:42.636 --> 00:05:47.516
it's not clear that squeezing
a little bit more out of the North Sea

00:05:47.556 --> 00:05:51.516
is more acceptable
than developing a new gas field

00:05:51.556 --> 00:05:53.396
in an African country

00:05:53.396 --> 00:05:56.116
whose past cumulative per capita emissions

00:05:56.156 --> 00:05:59.676
have been a tiny fraction of the UK's.

00:06:00.116 --> 00:06:02.036
We now have the technologies

00:06:02.076 --> 00:06:05.836
to dramatically reduce
the demand for fossil fuels,

00:06:05.836 --> 00:06:08.796
and we should deploy them
as rapidly as possible.

00:06:09.316 --> 00:06:14.716
That means ensuring that electricity
systems in every developed country

00:06:14.756 --> 00:06:19.676
get to zero emissions by 2035
at the very latest

00:06:19.676 --> 00:06:22.876
and ideally earlier, with no coal

00:06:22.916 --> 00:06:27.516
and only a very, very small
role for unabated gas.

00:06:27.556 --> 00:06:32.476
We should be aiming to reduce
coal demand 90 percent by 2050,

00:06:32.516 --> 00:06:35.556
oil demand at least 75 percent

00:06:35.596 --> 00:06:39.196
and gas demand by well over 50 percent.

00:06:39.236 --> 00:06:41.676
And we are running out of time
to do it fast enough.

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:46.036
LL: Those are some big reduction targets,
and we need to meet them.

00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:47.796
But as we move there,

00:06:47.836 --> 00:06:51.356
we'll still be using some fossil fuels
to meet energy demands.

00:06:51.396 --> 00:06:55.036
How do we do that while accelerating
our transition to renewables?

00:06:55.076 --> 00:06:57.876
Some people think we can work
with the fossil fuel industry

00:06:57.876 --> 00:06:59.836
and want to pressure it to do better.

00:06:59.836 --> 00:07:02.396
Others propose more radical departures.

00:07:02.436 --> 00:07:06.156
Here are a few opinions on this issue,
starting with Jérôme Schmitt,

00:07:06.156 --> 00:07:09.276
a former oil industry executive
who is now a senior fellow

00:07:09.316 --> 00:07:11.796
at the Columbia Center
on Sustainable Investment.

00:07:12.956 --> 00:07:17.036
Jérôme Schmitt: I'm not aware of any
net-zero scenario compliant

00:07:17.076 --> 00:07:19.476
with 1.5 degree or two degree

00:07:19.516 --> 00:07:25.436
that does not involve even
a little bit of fossil fuel

00:07:25.476 --> 00:07:27.756
by 2040 or 2050,

00:07:27.756 --> 00:07:31.276
especially for the developing
part of the world.

00:07:31.996 --> 00:07:37.796
Therefore, some people will need
to have an all-in understanding

00:07:37.836 --> 00:07:40.156
of the energy system

00:07:40.196 --> 00:07:43.476
and be able to manage
the complexity of producing

00:07:43.516 --> 00:07:46.236
and supplying to customers
and to countries

00:07:46.236 --> 00:07:50.716
clean energy on one side and less
and less fossil fuel on the other side

00:07:50.716 --> 00:07:54.236
and concentrate the fossil fuel
supply and demand

00:07:54.236 --> 00:07:58.076
on the absolute necessary means and usage

00:07:58.116 --> 00:08:02.956
and increase progressively
the shift from one to the other.

00:08:02.956 --> 00:08:09.556
Those who have the competency today
to manage this complexity

00:08:09.596 --> 00:08:10.796
and the balance sheet

00:08:10.796 --> 00:08:13.676
is still the oil and gas industry,

00:08:13.676 --> 00:08:15.196
whether we like it or not.

00:08:15.196 --> 00:08:19.116
So I would take the oil and gas industry

00:08:19.116 --> 00:08:22.356
as an opportunity to go faster,

00:08:22.396 --> 00:08:26.876
to make it right better
than we would do it without them.

00:08:27.356 --> 00:08:29.636
But if it doesn't prove possible

00:08:29.636 --> 00:08:32.716
because the oil and gas industry
doesn't shift and doesn't adapt,

00:08:32.716 --> 00:08:33.956
we must do without.

00:08:33.956 --> 00:08:36.836
Ramez Naam: Oil and gas companies
have to realize --

00:08:36.876 --> 00:08:39.316
they don't always,
but they really should realize --

00:08:39.356 --> 00:08:40.676
the writing is on the wall.

00:08:40.676 --> 00:08:43.636
We're spending close
to twice as much on clean energy

00:08:43.636 --> 00:08:47.036
as we are on fossil fuel
infrastructure already,

00:08:47.036 --> 00:08:49.316
and that gap is just
going to widen and widen.

00:08:49.316 --> 00:08:54.556
So I think if we can pivot
those companies, that industry,

00:08:54.596 --> 00:08:58.116
into working on a clean future,
it's a good thing.

00:08:58.116 --> 00:09:02.396
But we shouldn't have illusions
that it's going to happen by itself.

00:09:02.396 --> 00:09:06.916
The pivot that has happened has been
because companies have seen

00:09:06.956 --> 00:09:09.596
that the future is clean.

00:09:09.636 --> 00:09:12.716
And so a combination
of continuing to push on policy,

00:09:12.716 --> 00:09:15.156
on citizen advocacy

00:09:15.196 --> 00:09:18.756
and on driving for the technologies
that disrupt fossil fuels

00:09:18.796 --> 00:09:22.916
is what will cause those large,
entrenched fossil fuel companies

00:09:22.956 --> 00:09:25.076
to pivot towards the clean future.

00:09:25.116 --> 00:09:29.476
Tzeporah Berman: We're living the last
gasp of the fossil fuel industry,

00:09:29.476 --> 00:09:33.356
and they're doing everything they can
to weaken climate policy

00:09:33.396 --> 00:09:34.956
and hold on to the market

00:09:34.956 --> 00:09:36.956
because they know renewables are cheaper.

00:09:36.956 --> 00:09:40.796
MR: There was a great opportunity
to move with all the skills

00:09:40.796 --> 00:09:43.596
that the fossil fuel industry
has built up over the years,

00:09:43.596 --> 00:09:45.516
convert over to clean energy.

00:09:45.556 --> 00:09:47.476
That would have been a wonderful moment.

00:09:47.476 --> 00:09:49.956
If that had happened,
things would be very different.

00:09:49.996 --> 00:09:53.876
We don’t have the time now to get into
a long, transition-y kind of discourse,

00:09:53.916 --> 00:09:55.876
which is what most of them seem to want.

00:09:55.876 --> 00:09:58.956
They lack the urgency
of now in their talk.

00:09:59.756 --> 00:10:01.996
DB: As you can see,
there's a lot of skepticism

00:10:02.036 --> 00:10:05.636
about the fossil fuel industry
based on its history of deception.

00:10:06.116 --> 00:10:09.036
Here's well-known climate
activist Luisa Neubauer,

00:10:09.036 --> 00:10:11.476
urging us not to believe
everything we hear.

00:10:12.796 --> 00:10:16.196
Luisa Neubauer: People, for instance,
call the climate crisis manmade.

00:10:16.196 --> 00:10:18.316
And while there were indeed
humans behind it,

00:10:18.356 --> 00:10:22.316
it's much less manmade
and much more fossil-fuel-made.

00:10:22.316 --> 00:10:27.076
It's made possible by the exploitation
of coal, oil and gas

00:10:27.116 --> 00:10:30.116
and the profit-driven
economic systems behind it.

00:10:30.156 --> 00:10:35.276
Calling the climate crisis manmade
implies it's an accident of human nature,

00:10:35.316 --> 00:10:38.956
whereas it's actually
a relatively small group of people

00:10:38.996 --> 00:10:41.756
and just a few places around the world:

00:10:41.756 --> 00:10:43.156
the fossil fuel industries,

00:10:43.196 --> 00:10:45.676
their marketing and their
political supporters.

00:10:46.156 --> 00:10:50.876
The fossil fuel industry itself
is also a powerhouse of fairy tales.

00:10:51.276 --> 00:10:52.556
Fifty years ago,

00:10:52.556 --> 00:10:57.276
they knew that their business
would lead us into a climate disaster.

00:10:57.636 --> 00:11:04.356
They denied their own climate science
that they did themselves,

00:11:04.396 --> 00:11:05.956
and by that,

00:11:05.996 --> 00:11:10.196
they stole our very first
historic chance to act from us.

00:11:10.876 --> 00:11:13.756
So now, for many lives,
it's already too late.

00:11:13.796 --> 00:11:17.556
And this time they present themselves
as part of the solution.

00:11:18.756 --> 00:11:20.796
They call it transition.

00:11:20.836 --> 00:11:24.516
They promise innovation,
they speak of green growth.

00:11:24.556 --> 00:11:26.276
And it sounds wonderful.

00:11:26.596 --> 00:11:28.396
It is powerful, too.

00:11:28.436 --> 00:11:30.316
I would like to believe that, too.

00:11:31.476 --> 00:11:32.596
But I cannot.

00:11:32.636 --> 00:11:35.916
We do not have time for any more delay.

00:11:36.396 --> 00:11:40.396
So whoever tells us that they will
just need some more time,

00:11:40.436 --> 00:11:44.676
does not understand the very basic logic
of the crisis we are in.

00:11:45.316 --> 00:11:47.156
Back in Hamburg, when I was in school,

00:11:47.156 --> 00:11:50.356
we could get 90 minutes
to finish a math exam.

00:11:50.876 --> 00:11:54.836
The fossil fuel industry is, in a way,
taking that very exam right now.

00:11:54.876 --> 00:11:56.116
But instead of 90 minutes,

00:11:56.156 --> 00:11:58.356
they tell us they will finish
in nine years.

00:11:59.196 --> 00:12:00.356
Back in school,

00:12:00.396 --> 00:12:03.196
that attitude would have gotten me
failing my assignments.

00:12:03.236 --> 00:12:06.436
If fossil fuel industries don't listen

00:12:06.476 --> 00:12:11.316
when people and science tell them
to get out of fossil fuels,

00:12:11.356 --> 00:12:13.396
they should not be listened to

00:12:13.396 --> 00:12:17.836
when they tell us more fairy tales
about wanting to be part of the solution.

00:12:17.836 --> 00:12:21.916
If fossil fuel industries
get to make the rules

00:12:21.956 --> 00:12:25.996
about the transition
we so desperately need,

00:12:25.996 --> 00:12:28.356
we will not get that transition.

00:12:29.676 --> 00:12:30.836
LL: The question

00:12:30.876 --> 00:12:33.716
of whether there should be any
new expansion of fossil fuels

00:12:33.716 --> 00:12:34.876
is hotly contested,

00:12:34.876 --> 00:12:37.436
and there are passionate
points of view on both sides.

00:12:37.436 --> 00:12:38.796
With a powerful perspective

00:12:38.796 --> 00:12:42.356
on the injustice of banning fossil fuels
in developing countries,

00:12:42.356 --> 00:12:44.676
here's economist Vijaya Ramachandran.

00:12:45.356 --> 00:12:47.236
VR: The average Nigerian consumes

00:12:47.236 --> 00:12:51.396
about 133 kilowatt hours
of electricity a year.

00:12:51.876 --> 00:12:54.996
What the average American
consumes in four days.

00:12:55.036 --> 00:12:58.716
In Nepal, that number
is 231 kilowatt hours.

00:12:59.036 --> 00:13:02.116
In Haiti, it's only 37.

00:13:02.916 --> 00:13:05.716
So what does all of this
mean for real people?

00:13:06.636 --> 00:13:11.036
It means that they might have a few
lights at home or a small fan,

00:13:11.076 --> 00:13:15.316
but no refrigerator, no water heater,
no safe cookstove.

00:13:15.716 --> 00:13:18.156
It means that poor people stay poor.

00:13:20.716 --> 00:13:22.796
Now let's look at climate change.

00:13:23.236 --> 00:13:26.036
Poor people did not create
the carbon emissions

00:13:26.076 --> 00:13:28.196
that have caused climate change.

00:13:28.556 --> 00:13:30.916
The 64 poorest countries in the world

00:13:30.956 --> 00:13:35.276
account for less than five percent
of annual carbon emissions.

00:13:35.916 --> 00:13:40.276
Even India, often perceived
as a climate villain,

00:13:40.276 --> 00:13:42.036
adds about seven percent.

00:13:42.916 --> 00:13:44.956
Poor people are not to blame.

00:13:44.996 --> 00:13:49.036
Germany's coal plants emit
four times more carbon

00:13:49.076 --> 00:13:53.076
than Pakistan, Uzbekistan,
Lao and Bangladesh combined.

00:13:54.436 --> 00:13:58.476
Yes, energy, poverty
and climate change are intertwined,

00:13:58.516 --> 00:14:00.916
but not in the way most people think.

00:14:00.956 --> 00:14:05.236
Most people think that poor countries
need to moderate their energy use

00:14:05.276 --> 00:14:06.996
to address climate change.

00:14:07.516 --> 00:14:10.916
The reality for poor people
in these countries

00:14:10.916 --> 00:14:15.796
is that they need a lot more energy
to adapt to climate change.

00:14:16.596 --> 00:14:18.636
As natural disasters get worse,

00:14:18.636 --> 00:14:21.476
they will need more concrete and steel

00:14:21.516 --> 00:14:25.196
to build resilient homes
and schools and roads.

00:14:25.836 --> 00:14:29.356
As temperatures rise,
they'll need air conditioning

00:14:29.396 --> 00:14:32.836
and cold storage for food and medicines.

00:14:32.876 --> 00:14:34.836
As droughts become more common,

00:14:34.876 --> 00:14:38.916
they'll need fertilizer,
pumped irrigation, desalination.

00:14:39.436 --> 00:14:42.116
All of these responses to climate change

00:14:42.116 --> 00:14:44.996
are going to require a lot more energy.

00:14:47.076 --> 00:14:48.556
So the good news

00:14:48.556 --> 00:14:52.436
is that we don't need to fear
this high-energy future for poor people.

00:14:52.956 --> 00:14:55.556
All of this energy
that they will likely need

00:14:55.556 --> 00:14:59.316
will come from low-carbon sources
to a great extent.

00:14:59.356 --> 00:15:01.836
The abundance of wind, solar,

00:15:01.876 --> 00:15:05.876
geothermal and hydropower
in Africa and Asia

00:15:05.916 --> 00:15:08.516
means that a lot of the energy
that we are going to see

00:15:08.516 --> 00:15:11.316
will not likely increase
carbon emissions very much.

00:15:11.356 --> 00:15:15.956
So how can the US and Europe
be smarter about ending energy poverty

00:15:15.956 --> 00:15:18.276
and addressing climate change?

00:15:18.316 --> 00:15:21.356
First of all, this rich-world hypocrisy

00:15:21.396 --> 00:15:26.196
of more fossil fuels for me,
but not for thee, must end.

00:15:26.236 --> 00:15:31.716
Governments in poor countries must be able
to pursue a range of energy sources.

00:15:31.756 --> 00:15:34.716
Renewables will likely
be the lion's share,

00:15:34.716 --> 00:15:37.236
but there will be some
fossil fuels in the mix.

00:15:37.276 --> 00:15:40.876
Natural gas, for example,
is abundant in Africa.

00:15:40.916 --> 00:15:44.916
Natural gas has powered the energy
transition in the United States.

00:15:45.276 --> 00:15:48.116
Natural gas can fuel power plants.

00:15:48.116 --> 00:15:51.676
It is one of the best feedstocks
for producing fertilizer,

00:15:51.716 --> 00:15:55.676
and it is one of the safer
and cleaner cooking fuels

00:15:55.716 --> 00:15:57.796
for women in poor countries.

00:15:57.836 --> 00:16:01.076
Renewables can be paired with natural gas

00:16:01.076 --> 00:16:05.076
to increase the share
of low-carbon energy sources.

00:16:05.116 --> 00:16:10.356
No country, not the US, not the UK,
not in Europe, not China,

00:16:10.396 --> 00:16:12.956
has developed without fossil fuels.

00:16:12.996 --> 00:16:16.836
The United States has recently passed
historically large investments

00:16:16.876 --> 00:16:18.316
in clean energy,

00:16:18.356 --> 00:16:20.236
but also in some fossil fuels,

00:16:20.236 --> 00:16:24.356
taking a very practical approach
to its domestic energy transition.

00:16:24.356 --> 00:16:26.476
The United States and other rich countries

00:16:26.516 --> 00:16:29.396
should take that same practical approach

00:16:29.436 --> 00:16:33.156
when it comes to funding
energy projects in poor countries.

00:16:33.596 --> 00:16:37.996
Let's not impose unnecessary
and unjust restrictions

00:16:37.996 --> 00:16:41.196
on those who are least responsible
for climate change.

00:16:42.476 --> 00:16:44.756
LL: If the lion's share
of new energy production

00:16:44.796 --> 00:16:46.796
in the developing world
will be renewables,

00:16:46.796 --> 00:16:49.316
a lot of new financing
is going to be needed.

00:16:49.716 --> 00:16:52.596
Here's Rebekah Shirley
of the World Resources Institute

00:16:52.596 --> 00:16:54.796
on why that's not happening yet.

00:16:56.156 --> 00:17:00.636
RS: Africa is perhaps the continent
that needs least convincing

00:17:00.636 --> 00:17:05.036
about the clean energy opportunity
for health, livelihoods and economy.

00:17:06.156 --> 00:17:10.116
As the fastest-growing, yet least
electrified continent on the globe,

00:17:10.156 --> 00:17:13.396
most in need of power systems
that can help fortify

00:17:13.436 --> 00:17:15.476
against the onslaught of climate shocks

00:17:15.516 --> 00:17:18.636
with both abundant fossil
and renewable energy resources

00:17:18.636 --> 00:17:19.956
to build them,

00:17:19.996 --> 00:17:21.436
Africa's energy transitions

00:17:21.476 --> 00:17:23.836
and how to dissuade
the use of fossil fuels

00:17:23.876 --> 00:17:26.676
have become an intense
international debate.

00:17:26.716 --> 00:17:30.916
Many sub-Saharan African countries
already rely on low-carbon resources.

00:17:31.356 --> 00:17:33.036
Kenya, where I live,

00:17:33.076 --> 00:17:36.436
generates 90 percent of its power
from renewables like geothermal

00:17:36.476 --> 00:17:37.956
and hydropower.

00:17:38.276 --> 00:17:39.476
Even in West Africa,

00:17:39.516 --> 00:17:41.516
where renewable shares tend to be lower,

00:17:41.556 --> 00:17:45.196
countries like Ghana generate
over a third of their power

00:17:45.236 --> 00:17:47.276
from renewable energy resources.

00:17:47.676 --> 00:17:50.956
And countries like Namibia
are at the forefront of innovation

00:17:50.996 --> 00:17:52.956
on clean fuels like green hydrogen.

00:17:53.916 --> 00:17:58.156
Couple all of that with housing
one of the world's largest carbon sinks,

00:17:58.196 --> 00:17:59.716
the Congo Basin,

00:17:59.756 --> 00:18:02.196
and sub-Saharan Africa
is consistently recognized

00:18:02.236 --> 00:18:05.756
as pulling more than its fair share
of the global decarbonization effort.

00:18:06.716 --> 00:18:10.196
So if the need is urgent
and if the resources are bountiful,

00:18:10.236 --> 00:18:13.916
then why are we still so far away
from this clean-energy future for Africa?

00:18:14.876 --> 00:18:18.356
And what I've learned is that though
the world loves to remind Africa

00:18:18.396 --> 00:18:21.156
about its vast clean-energy potential,

00:18:21.156 --> 00:18:23.596
the financial flows
to deliver that potential

00:18:23.596 --> 00:18:25.596
remain troublingly scarce.

00:18:27.356 --> 00:18:32.876
Projects and businesses incur a number
of hidden compounding costs and premiums.

00:18:32.876 --> 00:18:34.956
Like the risk perception premium.

00:18:35.796 --> 00:18:38.516
Or the "paying back your US dollar loan

00:18:38.516 --> 00:18:41.396
in a constantly depreciating
local currency" premium.

00:18:42.556 --> 00:18:46.956
Or the "expected to deliver
conventionally high rates of return

00:18:46.996 --> 00:18:49.036
while raising your revenues from customers

00:18:49.036 --> 00:18:51.836
that earn less than a dollar
a day" premium.

00:18:53.196 --> 00:18:56.116
So international finance markets
are not appetized,

00:18:56.156 --> 00:18:58.356
and financial flows here remain a trickle

00:18:58.396 --> 00:19:00.596
despite a pipeline of ready projects.

00:19:01.076 --> 00:19:04.356
In fact, though 17 percent
of the global population

00:19:04.396 --> 00:19:08.236
and almost 90 percent of those
still without access to basic energy,

00:19:08.236 --> 00:19:12.356
today Africa accounts
for a mere two percent

00:19:12.356 --> 00:19:14.156
of global clean-energy finance.

00:19:14.196 --> 00:19:17.836
From this perspective,
we can see a key missing ingredient:

00:19:18.236 --> 00:19:20.276
international cooperation

00:19:20.316 --> 00:19:23.556
to deliver the finance flows
that Africa sorely needs.

00:19:23.556 --> 00:19:26.476
Because there’s so much potential
waiting right at the cusp.

00:19:26.876 --> 00:19:31.436
If local enterprise had access
to long-term, low-cost financing,

00:19:31.476 --> 00:19:33.956
like their counterparts
in other regions of the world

00:19:33.996 --> 00:19:35.596
can simply take for granted,

00:19:35.636 --> 00:19:38.396
then Africa's clean-energy future
would build itself.

00:19:39.756 --> 00:19:42.116
HM: A lot of renewables
technology already exists.

00:19:42.116 --> 00:19:46.116
The finance flows, especially to,
you know, lower-emission countries

00:19:46.156 --> 00:19:47.356
don't exist.

00:19:47.356 --> 00:19:51.556
These are the countries that need
to grow and growth needs energy.

00:19:51.556 --> 00:19:54.116
That energy does not
have to come from fossil fuels.

00:19:54.156 --> 00:19:57.356
CA: We often speak
with the acceptance of this tautology

00:19:57.396 --> 00:20:01.156
that no country has ever developed
without the use of fossil fuels.

00:20:01.156 --> 00:20:03.036
But that's not the whole truth, right?

00:20:03.076 --> 00:20:04.196
The whole truth is

00:20:04.236 --> 00:20:07.756
that no country has developed
without the use of fossil fuels yet.

00:20:07.756 --> 00:20:11.116
LT: You have to really
invest in the future

00:20:11.156 --> 00:20:12.716
of these low-income countries.

00:20:12.756 --> 00:20:14.996
And access to energy,

00:20:15.036 --> 00:20:18.716
really looking at the poorest
households in all the region

00:20:18.756 --> 00:20:20.156
is really an important thing.

00:20:20.196 --> 00:20:24.116
If not, the transition
will always appear very unfair.

00:20:24.156 --> 00:20:26.156
MR: The World Bank
hasn't been doing enough

00:20:26.196 --> 00:20:28.956
to provide that more concessional finance

00:20:28.996 --> 00:20:32.356
that brings in private-sector financing
and that, you know,

00:20:32.396 --> 00:20:36.556
really looks at what are the projects
that are ready for investment

00:20:36.596 --> 00:20:42.556
and takes risks because it's really
important to have a sense

00:20:42.556 --> 00:20:46.396
that Africa is part
of the clean energy going forward.

00:20:47.116 --> 00:20:50.836
LL: Global finance isn't flowing at scale
where it's needed most.

00:20:50.836 --> 00:20:52.196
How do we change that?

00:20:52.196 --> 00:20:55.716
Here's Zoë Knight of the HSBC
Center for Sustainable Finance

00:20:55.756 --> 00:20:58.636
to explain two major roles
that banks could play.

00:20:59.316 --> 00:21:04.156
ZK: So the most immediate,
pressing task that we have

00:21:04.196 --> 00:21:06.396
is to finance the transition, right?

00:21:07.396 --> 00:21:10.876
And banks are mission-critical
to making that happen.

00:21:11.236 --> 00:21:15.756
And the reason for that is because
we have two very powerful levers.

00:21:16.076 --> 00:21:20.436
The first is really to take
environmental impact into account

00:21:20.476 --> 00:21:22.876
in the financing decisions that we take,

00:21:22.876 --> 00:21:24.596
and we're working on that.

00:21:24.596 --> 00:21:28.716
And the second is to really
actively engage

00:21:28.756 --> 00:21:30.596
with the governments that we finance,

00:21:30.636 --> 00:21:33.396
the state-owned corporates
that we finance

00:21:33.436 --> 00:21:37.356
and really work through how they can
deliver their transition plan.

00:21:37.396 --> 00:21:41.316
So if we think about the next
10 to 15 years,

00:21:41.316 --> 00:21:46.116
the industrial landscape is going to look
very different than it does from today,

00:21:46.156 --> 00:21:48.516
particularly in the case of energy.

00:21:48.956 --> 00:21:54.116
And we know that that's taking trillions
of investment to make the shift.

00:21:54.436 --> 00:21:57.836
We need a trillion per year
by the end of the decade

00:21:57.876 --> 00:22:00.796
to have half a semblance
of being on track for net zero.

00:22:00.836 --> 00:22:02.516
And let me be clear,

00:22:02.556 --> 00:22:06.196
one of the things that the financial
system absolutely has to do

00:22:06.236 --> 00:22:11.276
is enable a science-aligned phase down
of the financing of fossil fuels.

00:22:11.276 --> 00:22:12.716
We have to do that.

00:22:13.156 --> 00:22:14.476
But equally,

00:22:14.516 --> 00:22:17.796
we need to channel the capital

00:22:17.836 --> 00:22:20.316
that is being generated
from the oil and gas industry

00:22:20.356 --> 00:22:21.756
for good purpose

00:22:21.756 --> 00:22:25.916
because we need the know-how of the sector

00:22:25.916 --> 00:22:30.196
and we need to make capital move
in a way that's necessary.

00:22:30.236 --> 00:22:32.316
So think about this.

00:22:32.356 --> 00:22:36.036
The International Monetary Fund
has just estimated

00:22:36.036 --> 00:22:39.236
that the Middle East oil and gas
companies will generate

00:22:39.276 --> 00:22:44.956
an incremental 1.3 trillion [dollars]
in revenues over the next four years.

00:22:45.596 --> 00:22:52.316
The banks and financiers can help move
that money into renewables

00:22:52.356 --> 00:22:59.316
in a way that will make us achieve
the net-zero ambition much, much faster.

00:22:59.836 --> 00:23:01.556
DB: In any discussion on this topic,

00:23:01.596 --> 00:23:05.516
it's easy to get lost in the argument
or focus entirely on the end goal.

00:23:05.556 --> 00:23:07.916
What's also critical
is that we need more planning

00:23:07.916 --> 00:23:10.116
to get us off fossil fuels rapidly

00:23:10.156 --> 00:23:13.116
and without major disruptions
that might undermine confidence

00:23:13.156 --> 00:23:15.236
in the energy transition itself.

00:23:15.276 --> 00:23:19.156
Here’s engineer and environmental
sociologist Emily Grubert.

00:23:19.836 --> 00:23:22.596
EG: If we succeed at reaching
international climate goals,

00:23:22.636 --> 00:23:24.476
the fossil fuel infrastructure systems

00:23:24.476 --> 00:23:27.316
that support about 80 percent
of global energy consumption

00:23:27.356 --> 00:23:32.276
will mostly, if not entirely, retire
over the next few decades.

00:23:32.276 --> 00:23:35.156
What I'm talking about
is big infrastructure

00:23:35.196 --> 00:23:37.596
like power plants and refineries,

00:23:37.596 --> 00:23:41.276
but it's also things like
gas stations and people's cars,

00:23:41.316 --> 00:23:43.636
stoves and furnaces.

00:23:43.636 --> 00:23:45.956
Fossil fuel infrastructure
is a complex network

00:23:45.996 --> 00:23:47.196
of high-hazard industries

00:23:47.236 --> 00:23:49.996
that need to be able
to continue to operate safely

00:23:50.036 --> 00:23:53.156
until the new system
is completely ready to take over,

00:23:53.196 --> 00:23:56.876
including during emergencies
complicated by climate change.

00:23:56.916 --> 00:24:01.476
Just letting these systems go away
would be deeply disruptive and hazardous.

00:24:01.956 --> 00:24:04.356
We'd expect disproportionate
harm to overburdened

00:24:04.396 --> 00:24:05.756
and underserved communities

00:24:05.756 --> 00:24:08.516
who can't opt out
from skyrocketing prices,

00:24:08.516 --> 00:24:10.356
abandoned remediation plans

00:24:10.396 --> 00:24:13.196
and declining access to energy services.

00:24:13.236 --> 00:24:15.756
This means ongoing fossil expenditures

00:24:15.796 --> 00:24:17.836
and a reliance on highly-trained workers

00:24:17.876 --> 00:24:20.876
in industries that might not
outlast their careers --

00:24:20.916 --> 00:24:23.836
with a simultaneous focus on phasing out
these same industries

00:24:23.876 --> 00:24:25.076
as quickly as possible

00:24:25.076 --> 00:24:26.276
to address climate change

00:24:26.316 --> 00:24:29.636
and a host of other
environmental injustices.

00:24:29.636 --> 00:24:31.796
This is why we need to plan.

00:24:32.316 --> 00:24:34.956
And right now, there's basically
nowhere in the world

00:24:34.996 --> 00:24:38.156
where these urgently needed plans
for phasing out the fossil system

00:24:38.196 --> 00:24:41.196
while phasing in the clean
energy system exist.

00:24:42.716 --> 00:24:45.756
When we talk about climate goals,
the question I like to ask is:

00:24:45.796 --> 00:24:49.676
If we actually believed
we were going to succeed,

00:24:49.716 --> 00:24:51.956
what would we need to be doing right now?

00:24:51.996 --> 00:24:56.356
One strategy here is to set deadlines
for fossil asset retirements.

00:24:57.156 --> 00:25:00.396
Ample notice, say, a decade,
gives communities enough time

00:25:00.396 --> 00:25:02.596
to create and implement plans,

00:25:02.636 --> 00:25:05.276
and a legislated end date
gives people enough confidence

00:25:05.276 --> 00:25:08.196
to commit to what can be
an intense process.

00:25:09.036 --> 00:25:10.756
I showed that in the United States,

00:25:10.756 --> 00:25:14.916
requiring all fossil fuel-fired power
generators to close by 2035,

00:25:14.956 --> 00:25:17.916
which is President Biden's power sector
decarbonization target,

00:25:17.956 --> 00:25:20.156
would actually allow
the large majority of them

00:25:20.156 --> 00:25:22.476
to meet or exceed a typical lifespan,

00:25:22.516 --> 00:25:24.676
potentially giving communities
some confidence

00:25:24.676 --> 00:25:26.396
that these deadlines are manageable.

00:25:26.436 --> 00:25:29.676
That is what earns
and maintains trust.

00:25:29.676 --> 00:25:33.396
Not far-off emissions targets
with vague implementation plans.

00:25:33.436 --> 00:25:36.636
The alternative of unplanned
transitions is unacceptable

00:25:36.676 --> 00:25:37.916
but common.

00:25:38.356 --> 00:25:41.796
Planning the transition is an ethical
responsibility that takes time

00:25:41.836 --> 00:25:44.516
but also creates opportunities
to implement a just

00:25:44.556 --> 00:25:45.716
and sustainable future

00:25:45.756 --> 00:25:47.916
that corrects the harms of the past.

00:25:48.516 --> 00:25:51.076
Collaboration and a laser-like focus

00:25:51.116 --> 00:25:54.636
on ensuring people have what we need
to succeed through this transition

00:25:54.636 --> 00:25:55.836
will be critical,

00:25:55.836 --> 00:25:59.076
particularly as the transition collides
with climate tragedies.

00:25:59.756 --> 00:26:03.436
What would we do now
if we believed we'd succeed?

00:26:04.516 --> 00:26:07.916
Manish Bhardwaj: Emily Grubert
said something which I'm going to,

00:26:07.916 --> 00:26:10.676
really sort of, take away,
maybe even teach:

00:26:10.716 --> 00:26:12.716
that plans build trust.

00:26:12.716 --> 00:26:14.476
Promises, you may not believe mine,

00:26:14.476 --> 00:26:15.796
I might not believe yours,

00:26:15.836 --> 00:26:17.876
but if we put something down,

00:26:17.876 --> 00:26:20.436
this is what we are going to do
year after year,

00:26:20.476 --> 00:26:22.116
then I think that can build trust.

00:26:22.116 --> 00:26:26.316
I think that's just a beautiful thing
that I'm carrying away from her.

00:26:27.556 --> 00:26:30.836
LL: Emily challenged us to act
like we're going to succeed.

00:26:30.876 --> 00:26:32.996
Ramez Naam, a technologist
and entrepreneur,

00:26:33.036 --> 00:26:34.876
believes we have a real shot at it.

00:26:34.916 --> 00:26:36.636
Renewables are plummeting in price,

00:26:36.636 --> 00:26:39.676
and there's opportunity to increase
their share around the world

00:26:39.676 --> 00:26:41.676
if we get out of the way of progress.

00:26:41.716 --> 00:26:43.116
Here's some of his talk.

00:26:44.196 --> 00:26:49.716
RN: Clean energy will win
on cost definitively,

00:26:49.716 --> 00:26:55.796
but only if we get out of the way
and allow it to be built.

00:26:55.836 --> 00:26:58.076
Let's focus on that first point.

00:26:58.076 --> 00:27:01.556
Let's look at the cost
of clean energy technology.

00:27:01.596 --> 00:27:03.716
In 1975,

00:27:03.756 --> 00:27:08.916
if you were buying a solar panel
per watt of power that it produced,

00:27:08.916 --> 00:27:12.516
it would cost you 100 US dollars.

00:27:12.516 --> 00:27:14.276
By 2020,

00:27:14.316 --> 00:27:20.316
that cost had declined
to 20 cents per watt of power.

00:27:20.356 --> 00:27:24.076
That's a 500-fold decline.

00:27:24.116 --> 00:27:28.316
This is unlike anything else
ever seen in energy.

00:27:28.356 --> 00:27:34.596
It is unlike anything else ever seen
in physical infrastructure.

00:27:34.636 --> 00:27:37.276
And it continues today,

00:27:37.316 --> 00:27:41.316
and it is likely to continue
for decades to come.

00:27:41.996 --> 00:27:47.996
Now, this has surprised
the leading experts on clean energy

00:27:48.036 --> 00:27:51.836
and even the biggest optimists
on the future of clean energy.

00:27:51.876 --> 00:27:57.396
We've seen the cost of batteries
that power our electric vehicles

00:27:57.436 --> 00:27:59.356
and grid energy storage

00:27:59.396 --> 00:28:03.876
drop at the same pace or faster
than the pace of solar.

00:28:03.916 --> 00:28:07.036
And we are just at the very beginning

00:28:07.076 --> 00:28:09.236
of an exponential cost decline

00:28:09.276 --> 00:28:11.756
in the cost of using clean electricity

00:28:11.796 --> 00:28:14.476
to make hydrogen and other fuels

00:28:14.516 --> 00:28:16.716
that we can use to power industry,

00:28:16.716 --> 00:28:20.076
to provide weeks or months
of storage on the grid

00:28:20.116 --> 00:28:23.756
and to provide fuels
we can use for aviation,

00:28:23.756 --> 00:28:25.676
for shipping and so on.

00:28:25.716 --> 00:28:29.676
Clean energy technologies
are technologies,

00:28:29.716 --> 00:28:32.876
and they drop in cost like technology.

00:28:32.916 --> 00:28:35.956
As they are scaled,
they come down in price.

00:28:35.996 --> 00:28:39.276
Meanwhile, fossil fuels are commodities,

00:28:39.316 --> 00:28:43.316
and fossil fuel prices
fluctuate over time.

00:28:43.316 --> 00:28:47.556
But there's two barriers
I want to talk about in particular,

00:28:47.596 --> 00:28:50.436
which are the reluctance to build --

00:28:50.436 --> 00:28:52.396
not in my backyard --

00:28:52.436 --> 00:28:55.156
and the challenges with permitting.

00:28:55.156 --> 00:28:57.596
Because we look at renewables

00:28:57.636 --> 00:29:01.836
and a common complaint is
they take up too much land.

00:29:01.876 --> 00:29:06.556
All of us want clean energy
infrastructure,

00:29:06.556 --> 00:29:10.396
but many of us don't want it
in our backyard.

00:29:11.236 --> 00:29:16.036
In winter you have one-seventh
the solar resource in the UK

00:29:16.076 --> 00:29:17.236
that you have in summer.

00:29:17.236 --> 00:29:22.356
You have one-sixth the solar resource
in Germany that you have in summer.

00:29:22.396 --> 00:29:24.436
So every model,

00:29:24.436 --> 00:29:29.596
every simulation of weather
and energy demand shows this.

00:29:29.636 --> 00:29:34.636
It shows that if you want to have
the highest reliability grid

00:29:34.636 --> 00:29:38.516
at the lowest cost
with the least carbon emissions

00:29:38.516 --> 00:29:40.876
and the most clean energy deployment,

00:29:40.916 --> 00:29:44.636
you want to build a continent-sized grid.

00:29:44.676 --> 00:29:49.116
In China, the bulk of energy demand
is on the East Coast,

00:29:49.116 --> 00:29:54.516
yet the greatest solar resources
and wind resources are in the interior.

00:29:54.556 --> 00:30:00.356
And China is building literally scores
of high-voltage power lines

00:30:00.396 --> 00:30:04.516
that transmit power
from where the sun and wind are

00:30:04.556 --> 00:30:07.236
to the coasts,
where the energy demand is.

00:30:07.276 --> 00:30:09.436
And the longest of these lines right now,

00:30:09.476 --> 00:30:14.876
that Ürümqi to Shanghai line,
is 3,400 kilometers long.

00:30:14.916 --> 00:30:17.996
It is 90 percent efficient,
very low losses,

00:30:18.036 --> 00:30:22.316
and it adds maybe a penny or two
to the cost of electricity.

00:30:22.356 --> 00:30:26.236
That's what's technically feasible
with current technology,

00:30:26.276 --> 00:30:27.676
let alone advances.

00:30:27.716 --> 00:30:31.436
In Europe, that would allow us
to transmit electricity

00:30:31.476 --> 00:30:33.676
from Seville to Copenhagen.

00:30:33.716 --> 00:30:35.836
To bring power from the North Sea

00:30:35.876 --> 00:30:38.436
to anywhere in the continent
that needs it.

00:30:38.436 --> 00:30:40.756
In my home country, the United States,

00:30:40.756 --> 00:30:45.796
we could bring power from the sunny,
wide open areas of New Mexico

00:30:45.836 --> 00:30:49.356
to population and land-dense New York,

00:30:49.356 --> 00:30:51.116
which doesn't get so much sun.

00:30:51.116 --> 00:30:54.236
And we could take wind power
from the Great Plains,

00:30:54.276 --> 00:30:56.516
that are largely devoid of population,

00:30:56.516 --> 00:30:59.036
and bring it to the coasts in winter.

00:30:59.076 --> 00:31:02.596
That is what a modern grid looks like.

00:31:02.636 --> 00:31:06.116
And yet we are not building this.

00:31:06.116 --> 00:31:08.916
It is time for us to build.

00:31:10.196 --> 00:31:13.116
DB: With technology advancing
as fast as it is,

00:31:13.156 --> 00:31:16.836
there’s certainly hope that we can
meet our energy transition goals,

00:31:16.876 --> 00:31:20.436
and there are ways to ensure
that everyone benefits in this transition.

00:31:20.476 --> 00:31:22.076
But it's going to be challenging.

00:31:22.076 --> 00:31:25.596
LL: There's no shortage of ideas,
but time is running out,

00:31:25.636 --> 00:31:29.276
making it all the more critical
to collaborate with urgency

00:31:29.276 --> 00:31:31.436
and resolve this fundamental dilemma.

00:31:31.476 --> 00:31:34.356
Here, again, are some leading voices
on how we get there.

00:31:35.636 --> 00:31:37.316
MR: We all need to do three things.

00:31:37.356 --> 00:31:40.276
We need to make the climate crisis
very personal in our lives,

00:31:40.276 --> 00:31:42.036
change our habits a bit as a result.

00:31:42.076 --> 00:31:45.236
Secondly, we need to get angry
about those who aren't doing enough.

00:31:45.276 --> 00:31:49.556
And thirdly, we need to imagine
this world that is round the corner.

00:31:49.556 --> 00:31:55.876
ZK: Every year I can see more focus
from different actors

00:31:55.876 --> 00:31:57.996
that are needed
to be part of the solution.

00:31:58.036 --> 00:32:01.516
LT: Renewable energy
is a good business in many places.

00:32:01.556 --> 00:32:03.556
HM: There is a strong
business case for it.

00:32:03.596 --> 00:32:05.476
It makes investment sense.

00:32:05.516 --> 00:32:10.956
HL: China is adding an astonishing amount
of wind and solar to its grid every year.

00:32:10.996 --> 00:32:13.876
RN: It's cheaper to build
a new solar or wind farm

00:32:13.916 --> 00:32:17.516
than it is to put fuel
into a natural gas plant or a coal plant.

00:32:17.556 --> 00:32:21.636
TB: Our job has to be to make sure

00:32:21.676 --> 00:32:25.196
that politicians know that they're going
to have the support that they need

00:32:25.196 --> 00:32:26.556
for the hard policies.

00:32:26.556 --> 00:32:30.356
LN: We need people to get on board
in a fight for climate justice.

00:32:30.356 --> 00:32:32.196
And this means getting people on board

00:32:32.196 --> 00:32:35.316
that have never spoken
to each other before

00:32:35.356 --> 00:32:37.316
and that have always disagreed.

00:32:37.356 --> 00:32:39.676
MB: Even at this conference,
you will meet people

00:32:39.676 --> 00:32:44.196
and you expect them to have
very particular sort of, polar stance.

00:32:44.196 --> 00:32:46.756
But when you actually
get into a conversation,

00:32:46.796 --> 00:32:48.356
you see the openings.

00:32:48.356 --> 00:32:51.276
CA: I love listening to people
with different points of view.

00:32:51.276 --> 00:32:54.756
Dialogue is absolutely essential
and so generative.

00:32:54.796 --> 00:32:57.676
But once we leave the safe space
of this conversation,

00:32:57.716 --> 00:33:01.956
we go back out into a world
that is rife with power dynamics

00:33:01.956 --> 00:33:05.596
and institutionalized,
structural inequities.

00:33:05.596 --> 00:33:07.956
And we have to just acknowledge that fact.

00:33:07.996 --> 00:33:12.276
RN: So I think it's not
a bad thing to listen

00:33:12.316 --> 00:33:15.196
and then apply your own judgment
to what you've heard.

00:33:15.236 --> 00:33:16.716
And is it self-serving or not,

00:33:16.756 --> 00:33:20.036
and what nuggets of truth
or what pieces of data are there?

00:33:20.076 --> 00:33:22.796
MB: What we don't want to lose

00:33:22.836 --> 00:33:26.636
is that ultimately there are people
at the heart of systems.

00:33:26.676 --> 00:33:28.916
And systems change will sometimes fail

00:33:28.956 --> 00:33:32.596
just because two folks
cannot get along with each other.

00:33:32.636 --> 00:33:35.836
Jade Begay: I think we overlook
relationship building

00:33:35.836 --> 00:33:40.316
as kind of cheesy or corny,

00:33:40.356 --> 00:33:45.916
but ask any person
who’s built change over time,

00:33:45.956 --> 00:33:48.836
and it happens through relationships.

00:33:48.836 --> 00:33:52.156
MR: I was very affected,
as were the other women leaders,

00:33:52.196 --> 00:33:53.516
when Jade said,

00:33:53.556 --> 00:33:56.556
"In our tribe in New Mexico, we say,

00:33:56.556 --> 00:33:59.876
'What if our best times
are still ahead of us?'"

00:33:59.916 --> 00:34:02.196
Our best times are still ahead of us.

00:34:03.236 --> 00:34:05.276
But so are our worst times.

00:34:05.316 --> 00:34:06.836
We have that choice.

00:34:06.876 --> 00:34:09.396
And of course, we have
to choose our best times

00:34:09.436 --> 00:34:11.436
and we have to know how difficult it is.

00:34:11.436 --> 00:34:14.236
And yet, Nelson Mandela famously said

00:34:14.276 --> 00:34:17.396
"it always seems impossible
until it is done."

00:34:17.396 --> 00:34:18.796
And I love that phrase.

